eep it in this time  period that's what it was yeah  we i thought about updating it um and  maybe having it about a filipino made or  something like that which would make  sense and there's a validity to that  but  um  the world of fashion is completely  different now to what it was in the  1950s  first of all it's ubiquitous the idea  that you would have to travel to another  country to buy a dress is is  impossible absurd not necessary  um  and secondly dior which is such an  important character in the film the  house of dior  was truly king in the 1950s in a way  that it never has been again  and  right now  we could probably name five ten brands  that are  the big fashion brands but there isn't  any one amongst them where you can say  this is this is the brand  whereas it was absolutely the case in in  the 50s that dior was  the brand and so that was i think very  important as well  but yeah too many things about  um the ubiquity of fashion and the  specialness the uniqueness of couture at  that time i think is why it had to be  kept to that period as well as  the class differences and the importance  of class many many  aspects of the story you know for  example that  i was under increasing pressure at  various times to cast a younger more  attractive mrs harris because that's  thought to be more commercial  but the story would have been utterly  spoiled if it wasn't about  a woman at that stage in her life so  there are many reasons why i think it  had to one had to respect the original  time and and age of the character  so yeah let's let's talk about your  actors um  what  made you  you know go with these two like  incredible powerhouses who when i watch  this i just  they make it look so easy  they  just they're so magnificent they just  bring all of themselves to everything  that they do  yes and they have extremely different  energies and very different approaches  to how they go about their work which  was really fascinating  and as a director you're always having  to adjust what you do to the individual  talent  so  this was actually a very very difficult  role to cast because  it's a leading role  but it requires a character actor and  there are very very few character actors  who can actually carry a movie  let alone  raise the finance for a movie  so to find somebody  who had those qualities is really only a  handful  and i'd always had my eye on leslie  because i thought she was very right for  this part and when she was nominated for  an oscar for phantom thread i thought  right well now's my chance  but even then i was told that it would  be difficult to finance it with her name  alone  um she has never actually been the sole  star of a film before so um  well since then she made that well no  she did um  ordinary love with liam neeson but i  mean that was the combination of the two  so for it to be her as the person  carrying it that was  it's her first time really  and so  um very luckily for me early on in the  process i had a producer called philippe  carcasson who produced coco before  chanel and more recently the father  and he has a long-standing relationship  with isabel and um he was six degrees of  separation you utilize it's who you know  everybody knows that about this business  so he spoke to her first of all  and she said you know  it's  i like like the script it's very  charming um but i need to meet the  director and i need to  find out who's playing mrs harris so she  leslie hadn't been cast at that point  and i met with isabelle and of course  when we and we got along very well and  we we clicked straight away  and  when uh we cast leslie that was the kind  of final yes if you like because  um paul thomas anderson is a huge  auteur director very revered and  respected in france and of course  isabelle  um had massive respect for  for leslie's work in phantom thread  well  leslie is known for playing it's it's so  interesting that you cast her in this  because i mean i saw her in la a few  years ago here  in long day's journey and tonight  which is one of the most difficult roles  for  for  women in the american theater is that  role  and i was awestruck so if you ever get  the chance to see leslie manville on  stage  it's really special  um  i don't know if i would have thought of  her for this but as soon as i you know  it came up and she was i was like she's  perfect for this  did you ever see mum the series the bbc  series i don't think so no anyone seen  it  yeah  so um it's a really fabulous series you  should try and get your hands on it and  she plays this  very quiet slightly put upon um  working-class-ish  matriarch who's lost her husband  and there are some parallels to mrs  harris but she's a very very kind  character mrs harris has a lot more  get up and go about her and she grows in  stature and confidence  with mum you never see her get angry you  never see her rail against the  appallingness of her family but there  was enough  in that character combined with the  astonishing performance in phantom that  i just knew that she was she was going  to be extraordinary for this did she go  back and look at any of her michael  movies when you were deciding absolutely  i mean i i  i really i first noticed her in another  year  um because that was an extraordinary  performance as well  um  and uh  and i  alan rickman was a dear friend of mine  and i and he they had worked together  she was in the original dangerous  liaisons uh on stage with him oh wow um  playing the um thurman character there's  an irony there which i won't go into now  um  but um  yeah so they he he was also very much in  favor and  and his widow in fact is the one who  said to leslie look um this director is  interested in you for this part you  should take it seriously it's really  going to be a good thing for you so  he became a sort of absent fairy  godfather if you like to the project  i also loved everybody that you had  that's that's in her world you know in  her world in england her friend vi  can you talk about finding a person to  play her best friend vai  yeah i mean this was one of the one of  the things i wanted to do so even though  i set it in its period i wanted it to  feel a little bit fresher and more  contemporary  and the character as written is just a  cockney cleaner like like ada  um  i think there was a danger  if everyone in her world was like her of  of of it being a bit  uh maybe a bit dull a bit cozy so  we  uh took advantage of the fact that in  the 50s there was a big  uh migration from jamaica to the uk  people seeking employment and a better  life  they're called the windrush generation  named after the boat that brought them  over  and so there was a great opportunity to  cast a jamaican actress as the best  friend um historically accurate but  feeling more contemporary as a as an  idea  and um ellen is just the most joyful  beautiful human being  and they have such a natural  they had a great natural affinity you  felt their friendship so strongly uh  straight away  and they're so different um and i just  love those contrasts and then archie  who again could have been a cockney was  was irish so that was a different flavor  again  um and then we had the jamaican bus  conductor  who comes back at the very end as a  possible love interest for advice so we  we were able to  make the world of mrs harris a bit more  diverse and a bit more interesting  i have a trivia question for you i  forgot i wrote this down  uh paul gallico  has had uh i mean i'm sure several but  another  very famous  screenwriter adapt him  into a film do you know who that was  are you talking about the poseidon  adventure  no this is pride of the yankees do you  know what what  screenwriter co-adapted  this was new to me i didn't i i didn't  realize i mean they just have this weird  feeling  gore vidal or somebody like it's herman  mankowitz oh wow i have no idea herman  makowitz co-wrote pride of the year no  idea also known as the lou gehrig's  story okay i'm not a baseball guy you're  in good company  as for people who have uh adapted we'll  know that now galico scripts yeah  um it's a good trivia question  i also wrote citizen kane just for  reference um  so  did you  talk can you talk about the look of the  film because sometimes you know i was  watching it and i was like this  feels like it came out of  i'm looking at something that was came  out of a magazine from this time period  was that something purposeful that you  decided to do with your designers and  your dp  so  the look of the film um had to achieve a  few different things first of all very  clearly differentiate between the two  worlds so i wanted london to feel very  different from paris  and we did that through  uh primarily through a choice of color  palette  so london  was mostly grays and greens and browns  and soft colors like that  and fog etc  and paris was sharper more defined so it  was black and white and more primary and  more punchy colors and we we accentuated  that with the grading as well to create  these two different worlds  i also  hired  one of my dear friends and one of the  most incredible production designers  that's ever lived luciana rigi to do the  production design  because like me she has lived in london  and paris and so she really understood  those two worlds and that was  particularly important because we shot  the majority of the film in budapest for  budget reasons  and also as it weirdly turned out  it had the lowest covert rates in europe  at the time  so we were able to get on with it when  many many productions were stuck  um  and so given that we were in budapest i  needed a production designer who knew  what paris  uh you know a doorknob or a english  window looked like because you know they  we weren't going to find that in  budapest we had to define it  and  in terms of the period feeling  i discussed with the dp the idea that i  wanted it to look a little bit like a  black and white film that had been  colorized  so that was what i was going for  and so we did experiment with starting  with black and white and then adding  color bit by bit the way you would in a  color colorizing process  but the other big decision was after  doing camera tests  it was very very clear to me that the  anamorphic lenses  which were made by cook in the 1950s  which had now been modernized  created a magical feeling by slightly  distorting the edge of the frame  and also changing  what would have been a round  light into a slightly oblong light and i  know that sounds  weirdly particular  but it adds to the sense that you're not  in the real world you are in some kind  of magical that's amazing  that's specificity  yeah wow yeah so that was super  important because the film is an  absolute tight rope between  reality and magic  and if you go too far into the magic  you lose your grip on reality and if you  go too far into the reality you lose the  magic so  it really was such a careful balancing  act between those two things  that's the thing that i loved about this  film is you know despite its being set  in the late 50s  it it was so easy for me as an audience  remember to  put myself in her to be in her shoes in  a mat because we all do imagine like  what if i got some money what would i do  with it you know what i mean not like  lottery  winner money but you'd like change your  life but something  that you could just splurge  and have a good time with yeah i really  think that's one of the absolutely  universal  aspects of the film because i doubt  there's a single person here who hasn't  aspired to something that seems  unattainable or saved for something that  that you know was really beautiful or  precious or special  and it doesn't have to be a an item of  clothing  um so the the i love the  the the actual dior show that she goes  to are those uh were those real  time period dior dresses  yes so i talked about the magic and  realism so one of the  um  the feat that the um the film dips into  is the real house of duo  and so i wanted to recreate it as  truthfully and accurately as possible so  we had the architectural drawings from  dior in order to recreate the house of  dior the interior exactly as it was and  we also  replicated the dresses because in my  initial  conversation with jenny bevin the  costume designer i said i want you to  out do you order i want you to  you know be inspired by him and create  something even more fantastical and she  said you're absolutely mad you cannot  improve on dior  so um  what ended up happening was very  interesting all of the dresses  except for the three invented dresses  from the book namely  revisant the first dress that she sees  in lady dance  bedroom  uh the green dress venus  and temptation which is the dress the  great dress that she wants those three  dresses are the book dresses  and they were all what i call beven dior  so they were all  enhanced  ideas inspired by dior designs  every other dress that you see is an  exact replica of dior including five  dresses that actually came from the  house of dior  that they had made themselves and they  they're the initial dresses that you see  in the show so the further you go into  the show  the more jenny bevan replicas and then  inspired by dresses you get but it's  very very that sequence is fabulous uh  leslie manville's clothes and it are  gorgeous  um  i mean everybody looks  so fantastic in it  it's so funny because i'm not a big  fashion person who like keeps up with  with modern couture but i'm obsessed  with fashion documentaries  and this was like watching a fashion  documentary it was just like the  opportunity to see gorgeous clothes on  amazing people yeah i mean we studied  those youtube documentaries about the  fashion shows and how the models walked  and the circuit that they made from  their dressing room into the salon  out in front of the staircase where the  journalists were sitting and then back  to their  either back through the room or straight  back to if they had a quick change to  make back to the dressing room and we  ran the show  as if it were a real show  and so we determined the sequence and  the sequence was based on  day wear and then cocktail dresses and  then evening wear ball gowns  and finally the wedding dress which was  the traditional ending of a show so  christian dior today  with their archivists and their  historians and their immense pride in  their heritage  have huge admiration and pride for the  film because it was extremely respectful  indeed a love letter to the house of  duel  so i watched an interview with leslie  manville today and um  she had some really nice things to say  about you so you should check it out  it's on youtube yes please she loved  working with you oh thank you  but she also  the interviewer asked her  what she what she hoped people would  take away from this phone what she hoped  they would feel and she said joy what is  it you hope that people will take away  from the film  um there are a couple of things i think  for me one is  a really central theme of my life which  is to never give up on your dreams  um  i don't think any you can have a  filmmaker in the world who  who isn't  completely dogged and persistent because  it's a very hard job to do and you  are often discouraged and think that  you're never going to get there and um  of course if if you do  bail you're not going to get there so uh  persistence and never giving up on your  dreams is very important for me and i  think for everyone and i i feel for  people who  who don't have the courage to continue  uh to pursue what they what they what  they desire and what they dream of so  that's one message  but also i think central to the film and  what attracted me to the characters is  the i think the whole notion of karma is  very central to the film  and the idea that  if you treat people with kindness you  will receive kindness back  um if you behave badly like lady dan  does eventually you will get your  comeuppance and you will  you will be punished  and that may be a bit pollyannaish and  idealistic of me but i think there's  enough truth in it that i'm i'm sticking  to that and ultimately i also want  people to feel uplifted because we're  going through a very rough  period in in the world right now between  climate change and war and  economic crisis and  if people can have a couple of hours  respite from that and come out of the  cinema feeling  really great joyful that's a wonderful  thing  did you feel great and joyful at the end  i did too  thank you so much for coming it's such a  pleasure to talk to you will we have any  questions from the audience or we don't  do that we don't usually know  thank you guys thank you very much  [Music]  you

